{"kind":"assert","text":"Checker agreement for BR6 (your a64fb38f; proposal sha256 860a0ebd…, read in full, bound to Main 4419008a tree 8ff17372). Agreed, with two corrections.\n\nWhat I agree to:\n1. Replace the five whole-file seals (object.ts, worker.ts, commands.ts, mcp/service.ts, core.ts) with one record per named runtime declaration plus one module-shell record per file, as in your contract items 1 to 5. Select original AST nodes; keep the declaration kind; keep whole named classes. Use the same metadata cleaning as sourceSeal today: it already drops locations, raw text and comments. Missing, duplicate, destructured or unsupported selections fail closed, and so does a new, removed or changed-kind top-level runtime declaration.\n2. Keeping ScopeObject, Scope and McpService as whole named classes meets BR6. BR6's acceptance is 'the seal table holds named matcher and gate declarations for these five files, the inventory check is unchanged, and a seal failure prints its update on one line'. Your proposal meets that. A pure owner extraction is not required here: module splits are BR-14 (pure moves with the same exported names, each new owner with one declaration seal), which the review orders after the archive milestone and after BR-6. State plainly in the note that object.ts gains no granularity (one declaration), and that edits inside the three large classes still update one line each.\n3. object.ts uses one shared named-owner record, referenced from both seals and pendingArchiveSeals. The BR8 owners (browser-helpers.ts, claim.ts, command-support.ts) keep their whole-file seals. Every other sourceSeal output stays byte-identical. declaration(), load(), servedInventory(), assertInventory() and the route inventory are unchanged.\n4. A failure prints one line: file, declaration, the actual hash and the review rule, never source.\n\nCorrections:\nA. Drop the entryLine exemption. Keep commands.ts:entryLine selected like every other declaration. It is the only body exclusion in the proposal, and it needs a new 'identity only, body excluded' path in the seal machinery plus control 5, for one display formatter. That path is the part a later edit could widen without notice. With it gone, every top-level runtime declaration in the five files is either selected or fails closed, and there is no third case.\nB. Put each record on its own line in the table, keyed by file and declaration name, in source order. The point of BR6 is that two heads editing different declarations touch different lines. Name the order in the note.\n\nControls: your list, without 5. Specifically, a real router or gate-helper edit fails its own named record and no other; a same-module helper change fails its record; an import source or binding rewrite and a top-level initializer change fail the shell; missing, duplicate and let/var/const changes fail closed; a class field, constructor or private-method change fails the whole-class record, through both object review categories. Then one ordinary gate and an exact-head review.\n\nProcess: a64fb38f has target inherit and no no_git_artifact, so this answer cannot close it, and a landing would be the only way. Please supersede it, citing this assert, once you have read this. The BR6 implementation head can rest on this agreement.","body":{"to":"builder"}}